WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.790
- Welcome to the 22nd
annual John W. Hall lecture

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.410
in Japanese studies.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.253
My name is Hwansoo Kim chair
of the Council in East Asian Studies.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.840
Last year, due to the pandemic

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.010
we had to postpone this event.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.670
And it seems like we
may be at the tail end

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.010
of this pandemic and
I'm extremely delighted

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.593
that we are able to host
this event in person today.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.220
For those of you who are not familiar

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.100
with this lecture series,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.763
I'd like to say a few
words about its background.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.630
The John W. Hall lecture
series in Japanese studies

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:46.630
was established with general
support from Mrs. Robin Hall

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.800
in memory of her husband,
considered one of these past

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.430
century's finest scholars
of the history of Japan,

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:59.410
John Whitney Hall was
born in Tokyo in 1916

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.460
and developed an interest
in Japanese language,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.460
culture and history at an early
age, after receiving his PhD

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.070
in east Asian and languages
and literatures at Harvard,

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.020
Hall began his academic career
at the University of Michigan

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:20.020
in 1949 and came to Yale in
1961 as a Whitney Griswold

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.470
professor of history,

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.993
a position he held until
his retirement in 1983.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.720
Professor Hall specialized in
the through late Toga periods

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.310
and throughout his career,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.880
he wrote or edited some
of the most important

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.960
and influential volumes
on Japanese history.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.170
He contributed to the study of Japan

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.000
through not only his writing,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.370
but also through service as chair

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.970
of several local and national committees.

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:56.970
Now I'd like to give the
podium over to Daniel Botsman

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.980
for introducing the speaker for today.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:01.813
Thank you.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.140
- Thank you, Professor Kim.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.800
It truly is a delight to
be in person with you all.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.380
And it is such an honor
to be able to introduce

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.230
and welcome back to Yale
Professor Noriko Mizuta,

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:24.290
a scholar who throughout
her career has given so much

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.370
to the fields of comparative literature,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:30.330
Japanese literature, Japanese
studies, and women's studies

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.220
and whom in so many ways,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.170
personifies the best
possibilities of the new forms

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.060
of intellectual exchange and
transnational scholarship

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.240
that were developed
between Japan and the U.S

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.203
in the second half of the 20th century.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.280
Before saying more
about professor Mizuta's

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.560
remarkable career and scholarship,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.590
I hope she'll forgive me

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:54.150
if I pause just briefly
to say a few words about

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.600
several other special guests
who we have here with us today

00:02:58.600 --> 00:02:59.433
because of her.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.290
So first, we're so grateful
to be able to welcome

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.150
two of professor Mizuta, five children,

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.303
Professor Akira Lipper,
would you mind standing up?

00:03:12.642 --> 00:03:15.240
(audience applauding)

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.340
Is a leading scholar of
cinema and media studies

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.150
at University of Southern California,

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.590
which I'm sure you know,

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:24.090
has a pretty good media
studies in cinema program.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.550
He is also vice Dean of the faculty

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.640
in the school of cinematic arts there.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.320
And he's written about
everything from electric animals

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.780
to Jacque Deda and David
Lynch, one of my favorites,

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.780
and he's currently working on
a book about Japanese cinema,

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.423
which I'm sure we will all
learn a great deal from.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:49.510
Professor Yuki Lipid is one of the world's

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.770
leading scholars of Japanese art history

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.143
at Harvard university,
like his big brother.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.070
Kio and I we're in
graduate school together.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.320
So I feel like it's okay
to use the familiar.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.060
But Kio's work too is
extremely wide ranging.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.720
But I will note in
particular that his book

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.940
on the Connell school of
painters from the early

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.810
Tokugawa period, in fact won
the John Whitney Hall prize

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.800
for the best book in Japanese studies

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.110
in the year that it was published.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.330
And that was of course
awarded by the association

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.763
for Asian studies to another
John Whitney Hall connection.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.860
We're also deeply honored to
be joined by Melissa McCormack,

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.030
who is the Andrew W. Mellon
professor of Japanese art

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.960
and culture at Harvard,

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.710
who many of you will know
through her brilliant

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.100
work on the interplay of
history, text and image

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.330
in medieval Japanese scrolls
and on the various forms

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:52.900
of artistic production
associated with the tale of Genji

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.810
and who studies of female
readers, writers and artists,

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.460
I think very much speak to questions close

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.973
to Professor Mizuta's heart.

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:04.260
Finally I wanna express
my deep, deep gratitude

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.493
to professor Alyssa Friedman.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.373
Who's up in the back.

00:05:09.373 --> 00:05:11.420
(audience applauding)

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.390
Who is professor of Japanese literature,

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:16.990
cultural studies and gender
at the University of Oregon.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.180
Many of you, including the students

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.440
in the Tokyo seminar that
I'm co-teaching this semester

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.523
with Dr. Michael Thornton,

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:28.470
already know what an important
scholar Professor Friedman is

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.380
from her wonderful books,
including Tokyo in Transit,

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.430
and most recently Japan on American TV,

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:38.580
but she's also editor in
chief of the U.S Japan

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.320
women's journal,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.820
which professor Mizuta
played a central role

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.777
in helping to found in the 1980s.

00:05:46.777 --> 00:05:47.860
And a few years ago,

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.550
when I mentioned to professor
Friedman that I was teaching

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.100
undergraduate seminar
about Yale and Japan,

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:58.233
she asked me if I knew about
Mizuta census 2004 book

00:05:59.722 --> 00:06:03.730
(indistinct), encountering
women's studies,

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.460
which included an account
of her experiences

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:10.890
as a graduate student
at Yale in the 1960s.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.460
I'm afraid to say that I had
not known about that book

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.710
prior to that this conversation,

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:20.440
but I was so delighted to learn about it.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.150
And I mentioned to Dr. Botel,

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:27.540
who had recently completed
his PhD in history here

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.850
that this wonderful book existed

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:34.770
and it would be so nice to
have a section of it in English

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.550
to be able to assign to Yale students.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.810
And he said, "Well I'll translate it."

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:42.390
And so with professor Friedman's
support and encouragement,

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.580
he was able to publish it appropriately.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.980
I think in the U.S Japan women's journal.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.240
And those of you who've
had a chance to read that

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.690
translation or perhaps
the original or perhaps

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:56.070
professor Friedman's
fascinating bio critical essay

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:59.260
in the 2018 special issue of the review of

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.230
Japanese culture and society,

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:04.290
that was dedicated to
professor Mizuta's achievements

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.610
will know that she came to Yale from Japan

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:10.110
as a Fulbright scholar in 1961.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:13.100
She was one of the first group
of students to actually fly

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.693
across the Pacific, to
come to the United States.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:21.470
And that 1961 of course was
the same year that John W. Hall

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:24.230
moved here from the
University of Michigan.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.653
So there's a nice connection
there too, I think.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.640
A graduate of Tokyo Women's
Christian University,

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.150
Tokyo, (indistinct) Mizuta's
original plan at Yale

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.890
was to focus her studies
on William Forkner.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:43.230
But her advisor here suggested
that perhaps she should think

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:46.240
about a different topic
and she ended up writing

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.500
her doctoral dissertation
about Edgar Allen Poe.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.110
I actually went today to
manuscripts and archives

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.180
to try and retrieve a
copy so I could hold it up

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.013
and embarrass you,

00:07:57.013 --> 00:07:58.790
but they wouldn't let me
take it out of the library.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:03.293
So I'm sorry, but it
is here in the library.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.350
And she later published a
book about based on that

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:13.120
dissertation in Japanese, under
the title of Edgar Allen Poe

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:18.540
after completing her
studies at Yale in 1970,

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.150
professor Mizuta hired
as an assistant professor

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:24.450
of American literature at
Marymont College in New York,

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.730
which is now part of Fordham University.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.890
And I think that's a pretty
amazing thing in itself

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:32.530
that someone who had arrived from Japan

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.750
just a few years earlier,

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:38.140
was appointed as a professor
of American literature

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:39.623
at an American university.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:45.000
She, after several years there
moved to take up positions

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.430
at Scripps college and eventually

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:49.363
the University of Southern California.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:54.260
During this period, she also
became increasingly conscious

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.950
of the ways in which
gender, nation and race

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:59.780
continued to structure and
limit the study of literature

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.210
in the United States.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:04.170
And she said about trying to change that,

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:06.460
particularly after her move to California,

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.900
she became centrally involved
in the establishment of

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:12.230
women's studies programs
that would help address the

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:15.113
exclusion of women from
mainstream academic research.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:19.840
And she also would later
play an important role in the

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.480
establishment of women's
studies programs in Japan

00:09:23.548 --> 00:09:26.470
and was involved among
other things in the creation

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:28.627
of the pioneering journal Feministo.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:35.130
She also in the workplace
became involved in ethics

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:38.550
to advocate for basic
rights for female academics,

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:41.330
drawing attention to the many
disadvantages and problems

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:44.360
they continued to face in the U.S academy

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:48.630
and with her friend and
fellow Yale, PhD Kioko Selden,

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:52.010
she also began translating the work of

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.410
Japanese women writers,

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:56.250
which had previously
beaten largely inaccessible

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.100
to English language audiences.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:03.830
This collaboration with Selden
would eventually lead to

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:07.370
multiple volumes of translated work,

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.930
which have now served as an
incredibly rich source of

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.580
inspiration for multiple
generations of students.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.820
My personal favorite is still
professor Mizuta's translation

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:20.063
of (indistinct) smile of a mountain witch,

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:24.160
from the original collection,
which was published in 1982.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.680
But I suspect many of you will have others

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:28.940
that have remained in your memories.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.050
Through all of this of course,

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:32.450
professor Mizuta also continued to produce

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:33.410
her own scholarship,

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:36.740
including her 1982 book
reality and fiction

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:38.640
in modern Japanese literature,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.010
which begins with the discussion
of the prewar eye novels.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.510
And then after exploring
the work of (indistinct),

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:53.210
it concludes by showing
how (indistinct) work

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:56.080
really served to transcend
the limitations of

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.920
the prewar eye novel in large part,

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.100
because she was able to
draw in aspects of her own

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:03.463
experience as a woman
and as a woman writer.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.580
Like John W. Hall,

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:11.900
she has also had a remarkable
career as an institution

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.570
builder in the middle of the 1980s,

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.810
she returned to Japan to
become vice chancellor

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:21.060
of the Josiah university
educational corporation

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:23.640
and threw herself into
the work of establishing

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.130
the Josiah international university,

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:29.053
which she became president of in 1996.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:32.170
Again, as part of her
efforts to build this

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:33.930
dynamic new institution,

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:37.410
she created opportunities for
female scholars and leaders.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.200
And although her own development
as a scholar was something

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.420
that emerged in the context of
post-war U.S Japan relations,

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:47.550
she has since built on that
foundation to establish truly

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:51.743
global connections, both
throughout Asia and to Europe.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.040
Even as she has held these important

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:56.760
and demanding positions,

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.480
she has also continued to
publish volumes over her own

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:03.940
poetry and literary criticism
and Japanese on writers,

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:08.150
including Sylvia PLA, Doris
Lessing, Orinoco SU Yuko,

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.220
and (indistinct) among
others and on topics,

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:14.440
including feminism
patriarchy in the family.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:17.550
In all of these ways and many others

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.830
that I have to admit today,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:23.640
professor Muzita offers us a
model for how to build a truly

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:26.590
generative scholarly career
that opens new intellectual

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:30.610
worlds while also creating
opportunities for others.

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:33.870
With regard to Japanese
women writers in particular,

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.040
I would like to believe
that the recent attention

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.630
that authors, including
(indistinct), among others

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.690
have garnered around the world
suggest that maybe the world

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:51.623
is finally catching up with
professor Muzita decades later.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:56.010
She has certainly been recognized
with numerous prizes and

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:58.290
honors from institutions around the world

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:00.090
for her achievements.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.530
And again, we are so grateful
to have her back here at Yale.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:07.400
Her lecture today is titled,
women writers and the discourse

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.480
of the postwar in Japan.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.830
Please join with me in
welcoming professor Muzita.

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:15.997
(audience applauding)

00:13:25.623 --> 00:13:27.680
- Thank you very much, Dr. Botsman

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.250
for your very kind introduction

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:33.520
and also ultra kind
introduction to my sons.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.733
Thank you very much.

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:43.890
I am very honored and
grateful to be invited

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:48.250
to deliver a lecture at
the John Hall lecture

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.170
at the Yale University.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:54.720
Before so many distinguished
scholars of Japanese studies

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:56.760
and in other field.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:00.460
It is truly a joy to have an opportunity

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:03.243
to come back to this campus.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:07.670
As Dr. Botsman mentioned very kindly,

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:11.690
that as a graduate student
at the Yale university,

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:16.690
I lived in New Haven from 1961 to 1966.

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:24.050
And my stay at Yale university
had a very important meaning

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:30.630
for my life because it marked
nothing but the beginning

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:35.830
of my second life as I scholarly career.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:40.550
But also I married here and
gave a birth to my first son,

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:44.290
AKI, who is here in New Haven.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:46.830
So it was a start of my family life,

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:50.540
and my experiences as a woman,

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:52.280
being married and having children,

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:56.363
and then their children
started in New Haven here.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:03.840
Also the time I came here
in 1961 was that time when,

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:09.283
and then until 1966,

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:12.680
it's the time when United States

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:17.000
went through very radical changes

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.250
in society and in culture.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:24.980
I will never forget one
day coming out of my class

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:29.350
and quitting the Hall of graduate studies

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:34.350
and walking on the street
and on the television,

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:38.160
I heard the announcement
of the assassination

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:41.570
of President John F. Kennedy.

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:46.480
And the assassination of
the president underscores

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:50.010
the tension that was
really becoming very acute

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:51.600
in the world.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:56.540
And Cuba had undergone a
very successful revolution,

00:15:56.540 --> 00:16:01.300
but suddenly Cuba was thrust
into the middle of the tension

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:06.300
of the cold war between the
United States and Soviet Union.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:10.873
So the threat of the nuclear
war was felt very palpable.

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:19.150
During that time, particularly in 1961

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:24.150
was the time of the beginning
of the civil rights movement.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:29.250
And of course it grew to during the 1960s

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:31.780
and then into seventies,

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:36.270
but 1961 was really
the start when students

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:39.870
and people started being
involved in the movement

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:44.620
and particularly school
students were on the forefront

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:46.540
of that movement.

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:50.040
I remember very clearly in 1964,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.173
my husband and my infant son,

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:58.150
we joined the March to
Washington in 1964 to hear

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:05.020
the Dublin Arthur Martin
Luther King speak.

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:10.170
And I was very much thrilled
that the historical changes

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:13.163
it's taking place before last.

00:17:14.590 --> 00:17:19.590
And that period also was the very dynamic

00:17:20.690 --> 00:17:25.690
and then active cultural
changes in the United States.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:27.840
And in granite village,

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:32.840
we could hear beatnik read
poetry and off Broadway theater

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:38.330
was very active, introducing
to American audience

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:42.800
many very radical works
such as the blacks,

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:50.390
and then Maad by Asad and
also the music free jazz

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:57.760
and so forth, gave me a feel that really

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.260
the dynamism of cultural changes,

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:03.390
and then also the sense of freedom

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:07.370
that is really taking
place in the United States.

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:11.080
I remember being very moved,

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:15.320
seeing John by singing
barefoot in the night club.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:18.273
And that was really a wonderful time.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.600
One of the events that I will
not forget that I experienced

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:32.490
during my stay in Yale was that
one of my very close friends

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:37.090
Taun (indistinct), who was
introduced by Dr. Botsman

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:42.090
just now, came to Yale to do her readings.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:48.310
And I organized the reading
because I knew how well in Japan

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:52.790
and also her books, her
poems were translated

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:57.070
and then had a very, not a very large,

00:18:57.070 --> 00:19:02.070
but a good audience in
English speaking people.

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:07.310
And the person who came
with her as a translator

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.037
was Kate (indistinct).

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:15.727
And you all know Kate's
(indistinct) work sexual politics,

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:20.570
which now is considered the

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:24.330
classic of the feminist literature.

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:28.500
But at that time she was
professor of English literature

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:30.150
at Barnard,

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.770
and it was before she transformed herself

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:37.000
into a very radical feminist.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:40.520
So it was a very interesting event for me,

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:43.740
knowing Kate (indistinct) as a person

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.490
who was really writing,

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:50.490
being engaged in writing that volume book,

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:55.463
the feminist criticism
of English literature.

00:19:57.170 --> 00:20:02.170
Now, I came to Yale
university in order to study

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:08.010
William Forkner because at
the time the Yale university

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:14.050
had so many really
young group of scholars,

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:17.530
and I was very much excited to meet

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:22.400
and take courses of wonderful professors,

00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:26.880
including Woodward and Robert Ben Warren,

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:31.880
and then W.H Jordan and then
John Holland among others.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:40.540
And I went to consult professor
Cray Brooks about writing

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:47.390
dissertation on folk, but
Dr. Crat Brooks mentions that

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:52.610
he just wrote a authoritative
book on William Forkner

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:54.810
and there's nothing left for me to say,

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:57.233
nor any scholars to say about Forkner.

00:20:58.893 --> 00:21:00.480
And instead he introduced, I mean,

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.140
he suggested that I do
research on Edgar Allen Poe

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:05.850
because he was very much under estimated

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:07.680
at the time in 1961,

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:12.060
no new books were written on Poe.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:15.580
And I was very, very
grateful for his suggestions

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:19.100
because it was really
the start of my research

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:24.170
scholarly research on Edgar Poe,

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:28.030
who has a tremendous influence
on Japanese literature,

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:32.117
through (indistinct) and so
forth, the French symbolist.

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:38.260
And also this was the first
encounter of a real prototype

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:41.360
of outcast in literature.

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:45.397
And then this theme of outcast
and in Japanese (indistinct),

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:52.240
has remained one of the central
themes of literary research.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:22:00.210
Now coming out of Japan,

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:11.110
being born in 1930s
and myself went through

00:22:13.063 --> 00:22:16.860
the period of (indistinct)
Japanese and Pacific war.

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:21.770
When I was a child, the
war was over when I was a

00:22:21.770 --> 00:22:25.110
third grade in the elementary school.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:30.110
And I went through my junior
high dates after the war,

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:33.540
during the occupations.

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:38.340
And that was a time in Japan
suffered from the lack of food.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:40.600
And even the starvation went through

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:43.220
and Japan was under the occupation

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:48.220
of the allied United
allied army based in Tokyo.

00:22:53.829 --> 00:22:58.829
And then (indistinct)
was fixed in order to

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:02.850
support Japan's economic development.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:07.850
So it was $360 per Yen

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:13.320
and Japanese to take only $200 a year.

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:19.040
So it was really a tremendous
experience as a young woman

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:21.150
to be out of Japan,

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:26.150
which was really confined in
its own territory and its own

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:31.520
thinking to come to Yale
and see now many, you know,

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:37.150
the young people who really
enjoy freedom of activities and

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:40.580
then thought in those days.

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:43.800
But at the time I came,

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:48.570
Yale was attended exclusively by men.

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:53.570
So sure enough, I did not see
any female student on campus,

00:23:55.260 --> 00:24:00.260
and also in graduate seminars,
women formed the minority,

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:03.530
for sure, but inherent had hall,

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:08.180
which was at that time I was
assign that's the graduate of

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:13.180
women's dormitory, there
are so many foreign women,

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:17.440
young scholars from all over
the world and then global

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:19.990
community was formed there.

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:24.270
And it was the first time for
me to meet Korean women or

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:27.740
Chinese women who are from
the neighboring countries,

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:32.740
but we Japanese didn't have any
chance to be in contact with

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:35.120
the people from the world.

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:40.120
And I do remember very clearly
those days that United States

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:46.000
was inviting many young
scholars to, you know,

00:24:46.810 --> 00:24:51.260
its own country with are
very generous scholarships

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:56.260
and also urged young people to go abroad

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:59.250
and to see the world,

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:03.260
for example, as a part of
the peace Corps, a group.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:06.890
So it was really the start of
the globalized of education

00:25:08.313 --> 00:25:11.350
of thinking and then
the personal exchanges.

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:13.680
And that was really a wonderful time.

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:17.290
And I was very grateful that
I had an opportunity to study

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:21.223
at the Yale university in those days.

00:25:29.088 --> 00:25:33.588
I would like to move on to
the theme of my talk today.

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:37.563
That is about single law.

00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:42.800
It means that postwar discourse

00:25:44.917 --> 00:25:49.407
and postwar Japan discourse, and well,

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:57.993
since the start of the
end of the war in 1945 on,

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:04.600
Japanese had been debating
and then furiously,

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:09.443
even fighting in the

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:16.640
understanding of the postwar, Japan,

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:19.970
in many terms in political and social

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:22.430
and then cultural terms,

00:26:22.430 --> 00:26:27.430
and that are really occupied field of

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:33.290
philosophy and thinking,
and then criticism in Japan,

00:26:33.290 --> 00:26:36.400
and then still now it goes on

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:39.630
and many people think that the postwar,

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:44.630
a period of Japan has not ended
and still will not be over

00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:50.650
in the foreseeable near future.

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:55.650
And simply because we
now in 2022 face a very

00:26:59.966 --> 00:27:04.733
serious challenge from the name
laboring countries in Asia,

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:10.760
from Korea, from China and
then east Asian countries,

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:14.820
Southeastern countries,

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:19.820
which were colonized or invaded
by Japan and particularly

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:25.450
the question of forced to
label and the comfort women

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:29.480
are the very, very hot
and then serious issues

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:34.470
right now in 2022, after almost 80 years

00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:37.900
of the end of the war,

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:42.900
and also the question of the
existence of the American army

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:46.420
base in Okinawa, as you know,

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:51.420
court is so much also the
problems and then also the debate

00:27:51.680 --> 00:27:53.000
among Japanese people,

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:57.210
which divided their thinking of Japanese.

00:27:57.210 --> 00:28:02.210
And then the direction is
coming this soon and the upper

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:06.210
house of Japanese (indistinct)
and that protest issue

00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:11.210
most important issue is the
change of the constitution,

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:15.190
which many Japanese
think that it was imposed

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:17.640
by the United States.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:19.200
And it was not the choice,

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:24.200
the own choice of Japanese
people to have this constitution,

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:30.240
which assures the peace,
a peaceful nation,

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:35.370
and also the gender equality for women.

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:39.593
So even though many
years have passed this,

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:45.590
we still debate that those
problems which are caused

00:28:47.260 --> 00:28:52.260
by the second world war and
probably goes back much before

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:57.970
are not solved unless
we face these problems

00:28:57.970 --> 00:29:02.780
and come up with very
reasonable solutions.

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:07.780
Then young people cannot go
ahead to think of the future

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:13.000
as a member of the Japan,
as a national entity.

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:19.500
So the post war period can be
considered in different ways.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:28.520
One of the ways to refer to
this period is since 1945,

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:36.570
that Japan surrendered to
the Western countries and,

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:42.710
to 1952, when the occupied
allied occupation was over,

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:47.650
that is suddenly the very
immediate since the concrete,

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:50.270
since the possible period.

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:53.140
But most of us, as I
have mentioned just now,

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:58.140
I think that the postwar
continues to go on in 1960s when

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:02.560
Japan experienced a
very (indistinct) rapid

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:06.780
economic development with
some environmental problems

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:09.240
caused by this economic development.

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:14.240
And also into 1970s when
Yen started floating.

00:30:15.724 --> 00:30:20.724
And then the foreign exchange
rate became the subject

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:26.050
of competition in the market
of the foreign exchange.

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:31.023
And Japan became economically
independent in the sense that,

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:37.400
that this protected rates of
exchange is now is thrown away.

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:42.190
And then oil shock came.

00:30:42.190 --> 00:30:45.970
And then after that Japan
experienced this bubble

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:50.070
economic development in 1980s.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:52.550
And then in 1990s,

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:57.550
we had a very shocking
event caused by (indistinct)

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:03.940
this setting attack on the
people of Japan in subway.

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:11.010
And these many critics and
then I think as in Japan,

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:15.660
consider that those instant mark,

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:19.693
the end of the post war
period, but nevertheless,

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:25.820
from the start of 21st century,
those problems are not,

00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:29.500
I mean, the problems caused by the,

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:33.340
the invasion of Japan and the
colonization of the countries

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:36.420
around Japan were not solved.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:40.263
And as I mentioned that the
comfort women problems really

00:31:41.690 --> 00:31:46.477
and important, important issues
that we cannot just ignore

00:31:46.477 --> 00:31:48.700
and forget about it and then go on

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:56.280
living in the modern period and
also this question of atomic

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:59.040
bomb and then radiations,

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:04.040
and then the damage that it
gives to human body and so forth

00:32:05.990 --> 00:32:10.990
has never been nearly faced
directory as a problem of Japan

00:32:11.650 --> 00:32:14.560
and also as a problem
of the United States,

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:17.440
I think which justified its use

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:24.780
to end the war and then to as say,

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:30.760
very controlled of the world
with the democratic thinking.

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:36.740
So I agree with these
critics who really think

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:42.740
it's important for us to
solve these problems in order

00:32:42.980 --> 00:32:47.980
to end the postwar discussive
conditions in Japan.

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:56.840
Let me just summarize the
point of the postwar discourse

00:33:01.060 --> 00:33:04.065
that were developed in these 80 years,

00:33:04.065 --> 00:33:06.640
almost 80 years in the post world war.

00:33:06.640 --> 00:33:09.500
One is the question of the responsibility

00:33:09.500 --> 00:33:12.880
and the paying of the
deportation to those nations

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:15.950
which are colonized and to
those people who are victimized

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:17.420
during the war,

00:33:17.420 --> 00:33:22.360
and many people think it
has no been done properly.

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:27.290
And still there are so many
people who are left and then who

00:33:27.290 --> 00:33:32.290
are very silent about this
being victimized by the war.

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:38.150
And of course, that comfort
women and then forced labor

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:40.523
is really at the core of the issue.

00:33:41.950 --> 00:33:46.950
And the second point of the
discourse of the post war Japan,

00:33:47.810 --> 00:33:52.810
it's the insistence of the
particularity of Japanese

00:33:55.820 --> 00:34:00.540
as Japanese people and also
the mentality, the culture,

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:02.783
a cultural tradition of Japan.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:08.660
So the (indistinct) as we call
it, which we had the book,

00:34:11.470 --> 00:34:13.720
the very in influential
book by (indistinct),

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:19.360
the Human Debations in the
Vertical Society, (indistinct).

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:26.540
And also a book anatomy
of dependence (indistinct)

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:32.893
by (indistinct) and there
other works written by Kaha.

00:34:33.820 --> 00:34:38.820
And they really talk about a
very uniqueness of Japanese

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:42.660
mentality in Japanese culture,

00:34:42.660 --> 00:34:46.400
which are not so easily destroyed
by the occupation of the

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:48.660
United States or the defeat of the war.

00:34:48.660 --> 00:34:51.190
It has a much deep rooted

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:57.400
the basis origins that Japan
built up during the long period

00:34:58.010 --> 00:35:00.463
of its own development.

00:35:01.910 --> 00:35:06.910
And together with this
particularity of Japanese mentality

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:09.400
and Japanese culture,

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:14.400
people started deceiving the
kind of criticism of this

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:19.670
particularity by say, the
Korean Japanese writers that

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:28.360
the particularities and then a
uniqueness are not on genuine

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:35.940
qualities, but were based on
the victimization of the others

00:35:38.270 --> 00:35:42.193
who do not belong in Japanese culture.

00:35:43.379 --> 00:35:48.250
And also the point of this
discussion is occupied by

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:53.250
so called (indistinct),
that twisted the phenomenon

00:35:54.830 --> 00:35:59.830
of a Japanese really mind
and particularly very evident

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:02.923
in the post war period.

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:08.650
And the important debate that

00:36:08.650 --> 00:36:12.220
are developed between (indistinct)

00:36:19.532 --> 00:36:24.449
the view of this (indistinct) very clearly

00:36:26.920 --> 00:36:31.830
that Yomo thinks that fear is responsible

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:36.830
for those who are killed in
the war, in that as a survivor,

00:36:37.340 --> 00:36:42.050
of this tremendous Imperial regime

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:46.380
that led Japan into the war and victimized

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:47.760
many young people.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:52.470
And he grew up as a young man
believing in this ideology

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:57.470
and was ready to face his
death and to feel very guilty

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:01.640
about what he went through as a young man.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:06.640
So he tries to go into the
analysis of a very complete

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:13.080
analysis of what led Japan to
have this extra ordinary act

00:37:15.230 --> 00:37:19.580
of getting into the second
world war and the forced Japan

00:37:19.580 --> 00:37:24.367
into the competition of the
colonial politics of the world.

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:30.370
On the other hand,
(indistinct), as you all know,

00:37:30.370 --> 00:37:34.750
are very ashamed of the Japan's defeat.

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:38.140
